# feature-nilpy-stdlib-coverage-gaps-measured — three container names.
#
# `set.symmetric_difference`, `set.isdisjoint` and the TWO-ARGUMENT
# `dict.fromkeys(seq, value)` were all compile errors. Loud, so nothing computed
# a wrong answer; the value is that a script reaching for them now runs.
#
# The first two are thin over operator forms that already existed (pyset_xor,
# pyset_and), so the gap was the METHOD name rather than the algorithm.
#
# fromkeys' two-arg form is a genuine Pascal OVERLOAD, reached because the
# stdlib call site re-targets by ARITY -- the route that site's own comment
# recommends. Both arities are asserted, since the one-arg form already worked
# and an overload that fails to register would silently keep answering it.
#
# The EMPTY-set edges are asserted for both new set methods: symmetric
# difference with an empty set is the original, and isdisjoint with an empty set
# is True (vacuously) rather than False.
#
# Expectations are CPython's own output.

s1 = {1, 2, 3}
print("symdiff ", sorted(s1.symmetric_difference({3, 4})))
print("symdiff2", sorted(s1.symmetric_difference(set())))
print("disjoint", s1.isdisjoint({7}), s1.isdisjoint({1}), s1.isdisjoint(set()))
print("fromkeys", sorted(dict.fromkeys(["p", "q"]).items()))
print("fromkeyv", sorted(dict.fromkeys(["p", "q"], 0).items()))
print("fromkeys2", sorted(dict.fromkeys(["a"], "z").items()))
print("controls", sorted(s1.union({4})), sorted(s1.difference({1})), s1.issubset({1,2,3,4}))
